<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Siddis</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Siddhi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Siddis are a community of African descent in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-asia">South Asia</a>. On the western coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, Siddi rulers of Janjira maintained a naval power. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha Empire</a> note that Maratha fleets challenged Siddi and Portuguese maritime forces along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konkan-coast">Konkan coast</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/siddis.jpg" alt="Siddis" width="320" height="200" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nagarjun Kandukuru from Bangalore, India · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Siddi_community_(16527139018).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Siddi, also called Sheedi, Sidi or Siddhi, are an ethnic group living in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. They are primarily descended from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantu-peoples">Bantu peoples</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zanj">Zanj</a> coast in Southeast Africa, most of whom came to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a> through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> slave trade. Others arrived as merchants, sailors, indentured servants and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>. Most were or became Muslims.</p><p>The origin of the name is disputed: one theory derives it from the Arabic sahibi, another from Sayyid, the title of the captains of the Arab vessels that first brought settlers. The first Siddis are thought to have arrived in 628 CE at the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bharuch">Bharuch</a>, and others, called Zanjis, came as soldiers with Muhammad bin Qasim's Arab army in 712 CE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Some Siddis escaped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> and founded communities in forested areas, and others were brought as slaves by the Deccan Sultanates, adopted Deccani Muslim culture and identified with the Deccani faction against Iranian Shia immigrants. Several former slaves rose to high rank in the military and administration, most prominently <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malik-ambar">Malik Ambar</a>.</p><p>African presence in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a> is documented from 711, but large-scale importation took place from the late 18th to the mid-19th century, during the Omani-Arab slave trade: captives mostly from modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kenya">Kenya</a> and Tanzania were sold at Zanzibar and shipped to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscat">Muscat</a> and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karachi">Karachi</a>. Hoshu Sheedi died fighting the British at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyderabad-sindh">Hyderabad</a> in 1843 and is remembered as a hero. After the British defeated the Talpurs they banned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> in Sindh. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balochistan">Balochistan</a> the ruler of Kalat formally abolished slavery in 1914, though domestic slavery persisted until the late 1950s.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> the Siddi are recognised as a scheduled tribe in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goa">Goa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daman">Daman</a> and Diu. A plurality of those in Karnataka are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a> (41.8 percent), followed by Muslims and Christians, while about 98.7 percent of those in Gujarat are Muslims. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> they live mainly along the Makran coast and in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a>, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karachi">Karachi</a> chiefly in Lyari, where many have traditionally worked as fishermen and sailors and in the port. They speak local languages, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balochi-language">Balochi</a> and Sindhi, and many have assimilated into the wider <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baloch">Baloch</a> identity.</p><p>They have kept ancestral practices especially in music and dance. The Goma, also called Dhamal in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, derives from the Bantu ngoma, meaning a drum, and at its climax some dancers are believed to carry the presence of past Siddi saints. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> the annual Sheedi Mela festival, with a visit to the sacred alligators at Mangopir, is the main event of the community's calendar.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Siddi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The court and army drew on three main groups: Persian, Turkish and Arab immigrants known as Westerners (gharibs), Deccani Muslims long settled in the region, and Africans, called <b>Habshis</b>, many of whom had arrived as slaves and rose to high command.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bijapur-sultanate">Bijapur Sultanate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Marathas also built an effective navy, with sea forts such as Sindhudurg and Vijaydurg, to defend the Konkan coast against the Portuguese and the <b>Siddis</b> of Janjira; under Kanhoji Angre in the early 18th century it challenged even the English and Dutch.</blockquote><a href="/empire/maratha-empire">Maratha Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>It later passed to the <b>Siddis</b> of Janjira, the African-descended naval chiefs of the Konkan coast, who kept it as a distant dependency.</blockquote><a href="/state/jafrabad-state">Jafrabad State</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Siddis</b>, Muslims of East African (Habshi) origin, held the island fort of Janjira off Murud as officers of the Ahmadnagar Sultanate; tradition dates their control to 1489.</blockquote><a href="/state/janjira-state">Janjira State</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Siddis</b>, of East African descent, ruled the island fortress of Janjira on the Konkan coast.</blockquote><a href="/state/sachin-state">Sachin State</a></li></ul></section>
